Two line feeds in the current token are the main signal. Its attached
punctuation determines the strength: sentence/continuation punctuation is
weaker than code delimiters or a bare blank line. Uppercase headings and
lines ending in ">" strengthen the signal. Ordinary text is near zero,
with a small word-versus-punctuation bias at the start of a document.
Only the current line and whether a line break has occurred are remembered;
these reset at document boundaries. The constants are training-data means
and small fitted corrections, not a token-identity lookup table.
"""
import math

READS = ()


def paragraph_strength(token, line, first, had_break):
    body = token.replace('\r\n', '\n').split('\n', 1)[0].strip()
    ending = body.strip('"\'“”‘’')
